od \\\\u2014 and separates what the plant takes in through its roots from what it makes. The contrast section puts the producer idea against the soil-as-food and water-as-food readings on the same plant so the boundary is visible, and the close checks whether the learner now recognizes the plant, not the soil or the water, as the maker of the food.\"\\n    },\\n    \"neighbours\": [\\n      {\\n        \"kind\": \"paragraph\",\\n        \"reason\": \"Present the concrete windowsill plant beside the cup of water so the learner holds a specific case before committing to a guess.\",\\n        \"role\": \"orientation\",\\n        \"teaching_block_id\": \"orient-b1\"\\n      },\\n      {\\n        \"kind\": \"heading\",\\n        \"reason\": \"Signal the category term \\'producer\\' so the definition is easy to locate and reuse.\",\\n        \"role\": \"definition\",\\n        \"teaching_block_id\": \"explain-b1\"\\n      },\\n      {\\n        \"kind\": \"paragraph\",\\n        \"reason\": \"State the producer definition as a living thing that makes its own food and apply it plainly to the windowsill plant.\",\\n        \"role\": \"definition\",\\n        \"teaching_block_id\": \"explain-b1\"\\n      }\\n    ],\\n    \"objective\": \"Recognize that plants are producers that make their own food\",\\n    \"reason\": \"Pose the initial commitment question about where the plant\\'s food comes from, without naming soil, water, or sunlight as food.\",\\n    \"role\": \"practice-cue\",\\n    \"stage\": \"document_writing\",\\n    \"teaching_block\": {\\n      \"brief\": \"Present the concrete case: a potted classroom plant on the windowsill, sitting next to a cup of water. Ask learners where they think the plant\\'s food comes from and have them commit to one answer before anything is explained. Do not name soil, water, or sunlight as food, and do not state the target idea that the plant makes its own food.\",\\n      \"evidence\": \"The objective\\'s recognition claim only be