d comes from, so a personal wrong model is on the table. The explanation then fixes the category term \\\\u2014 the plant is a producer, a living thing that makes its own food \\\\u2014 and separates what the plant takes in through its roots from what it makes. The contrast section puts the producer idea against the soil-as-food and water-as-food readings on the same plant so the boundary is visible, and the close checks whether the learner now recognizes the plant, not the soil or the water, as the maker of the food.\"\\n    },\\n    \"neighbours\": [\\n      {\\n        \"kind\": \"paragraph\",\\n        \"reason\": \"Present the concrete windowsill plant beside the cup of water so the learner holds a specific case before committing to a guess.\",\\n        \"role\": \"orientation\",\\n        \"teaching_block_id\": \"orient-b1\"\\n      },\\n      {\\n        \"kind\": \"callout\",\\n        \"reason\": \"Pose the initial commitment question about where the plant\\'s food comes from, without naming soil, water, or sunlight as food.\",\\n        \"role\": \"practice-cue\",\\n        \"teaching_block_id\": \"orient-b1\"\\n      },\\n      {\\n        \"kind\": \"paragraph\",\\n        \"reason\": \"State the producer definition as a living thing that makes its own food and apply it plainly to the windowsill plant.\",\\n        \"role\": \"definition\",\\n        \"teaching_block_id\": \"explain-b1\"\\n      },\\n      {\\n        \"kind\": \"paragraph\",\\n        \"reason\": \"Separate what the plant takes in through its roots from what the plant makes, keeping attention on the plant as maker.\",\\n        \"role\": \"explanation\",\\n        \"teaching_block_id\": \"explain-b2\"\\n      }\\n    ],\\n    \"objective\": \"Recognize that plants are producers that make their own food\",\\n    \"reason\": \"Signal the category term \\'producer\\' so the definition is easy to locate and reuse.\",\\n    \"role\": \"definition\",\\n    \"stage\": \"document_writing\",\\n