d fields.\\\\n- No component_id, template_id, page_break, ruled_lines, or media objects.\\\\n- Do not author interactions; those use the interaction writer.\\\\n- The Teaching Plan brief is a WRITER INSTRUCTION, not student-facing text.\\\\n  Never copy the brief verbatim into the learner-facing fields.\\\\n- Honour objective, terminology, evidence / approved facts, and must_not_introduce.\\\\n- Do not reteach prior_established content; reference it briefly when useful.\\\\n- Stay coherent with neighbouring composition choices when provided.\\\\n- Do not invent placeholder rows (\\\\\"Row 1\\\\\"), generic headers (\\\\\"Item\\\\\"/\\\\\"Detail\\\\\"),\\\\n  or \\\\\"Content pending.\\\\\"\\\\n\\\\nPrimitive field contracts:\\\\n- paragraph \\\\u2192 { \\\\\"text\\\\\": string }\\\\n- heading \\\\u2192 { \\\\\"text\\\\\": string, \\\\\"level\\\\\": 1|2|3 }\\\\n- list \\\\u2192 { \\\\\"ordered\\\\\": boolean, \\\\\"items\\\\\": [string, ...] }\\\\n- table \\\\u2192 { \\\\\"headers\\\\\": [string, ...], \\\\\"rows\\\\\": [[string, ...], ...], \\\\\"caption\\\\\": string }\\\\n- callout \\\\u2192 { \\\\\"tone\\\\\": \\\\\"note\\\\\"|\\\\\"warning\\\\\"|\\\\\"tip\\\\\"|\\\\\"important\\\\\", \\\\\"title\\\\\": string, \\\\\"body\\\\\": string }\\\\n- figure \\\\u2192 { \\\\\"caption\\\\\": string, \\\\\"alt\\\\\": string }\\\\n\\\\nReturn JSON matching the assigned primitive schema only (plus kind).\\\\n\",\\n  \"knowledge\": {},\\n  \"modes\": [\\n    \"generate\"\\n  ],\\n  \"native_path\": \"document\",\\n  \"payload_schema\": {\\n    \"additionalProperties\": false,\\n    \"properties\": {\\n      \"kind\": {\\n        \"const\": \"paragraph\"\\n      },\\n      \"text\": {\\n        \"minLength\": 1,\\n        \"type\": \"string\"\\n      }\\n    },\\n    \"required\": [\\n      \"kind\",\\n      \"text\"\\n    ],\\n    \"type\": \"object\"\\n  },\\n  \"required_inputs\": [\\n    \"kind\",\\n    \"brief\",\\n    \"teaching_block\"\\n  ],\\n  \"validator_refs\": [\\n    \"document.writer_schema\"\\n  ]\\n}\\n\\n## SCOPED REQUEST\\n{\\n  \"inputs\": {\\n    \"allowed_facts\": [\\n      \"plants have roots, stems and leaves\",\\n      \"living things need food to grow\",\\n      \"Plants are living things that can produce their own food\",\\n      \"Plants are called producers\",\\n      \"Use one concrete classroom example.\",\\n      \"Explain that plants make their own food (are producers)\",\\n      \"Identify leaves as the site of photosynthesis and chlorophyll as the light-capturing substance\",\\n      \"Describe photosynthesis as using light, carbon dioxide, and water to produce food and oxygen\",\\n      \"Explain why light is essential for photosynthesis\"\\n    ],\\n    \"brief\": \"Take the windowsill plant and put the soil-as-food reading beside the target: one learner says the plant\\'s food is the soil, pulled up through the roots; the producer idea says the plant makes its own food and only draws materials from the soil. Show why the soil reading cannot stand, since the plant still makes food inside itself and the soil is only one of its intakes. Do not repeat the earlier intake-versus-made distinction in full, and do not slide into the water-as-food reading here.\",\\n    \"kind\": \"paragraph\",\\n    \"lesson_context\": {\\n      \"input_availability\": \"supplied_statements\",\\n      \"objective\": \"Recognize that plants are producers that make their own food\",\\n      \"subject\": \"Science\",\\n      \"teaching_plan_revision\": 1,\\n      \"title\": \"The lesson opens on the classroom plant on the windowsill beside the cup of water and asks learners to commit to a guess about where its food comes from, so a personal wrong model is on the table. The explanation then fixes the category term \\\\u2014 the plant is a producer, a living thing that makes its own food \\\\u2014 and separates what the plant takes in through its roots from what it makes. The contrast section puts the producer idea against the soil-as-food and water-as-food readings on the same plant so the boundary is visible, and the close checks whether the learner now recognizes the plant, not the soil or the water, as the maker of the food.\"\\n    },\\n    \"neighbours\": [\\n      {\\n        \"kind\": \"paragraph\",\\n        \"reason\": \"Separate what the plant takes in through its roots from what the plant makes, keeping attention on the plant as maker.\",\\n        \"role\": \"explanation\",\\n        \"teaching_block_id\": \"explain-b2\"\\n      },\\n      {\\n        \"kind\": \"figure\",\\n        \"reason\": \"Show intake through roots contrasted with food made inside the plant, reinforcing the intake-versus-made distinction without introducing new content.\",\\n        \"role\": \"illustration\",\\n        \"teaching_block_id\": \"explain-b2\"\\n      },\\n      {\\n        \"kind\": \"callout\",\\n        \"reason\": \"Ask the learner to select which reading of the windowsill plant matches the producer idea, surfacing any remaining soil-as-food model.\",\\n        \"role\": \"practice-cue\",\\n        \"teaching_block_id\": \"contrast-b1\"\\n      },\\n      {\\n        \"kind\": \"paragraph\",\\n        \"reason\": \"Set the water-as-food reading against the target on the same windowsill plant and cup of water already in view.\",\\n        \"role\": \"misconception\",\\n        \"teaching_block_id\": \"contrast-b2\"\\n      }\\n    ],\\n    \"reason\": \"Surface the soil-as-food reading beside the producer idea so the learner sees why the soil reading cannot stand.\",\\n    \"role\": \"misconception\",\\n    \"teaching_block\": {\\n      \"brief\": \"Take the windowsill plant and put the soil-as-food reading beside the target: one learner says the plant\\'s food is the soil, pulled up through the roots; the producer idea says the plant makes its own food and only draws materials from the soil. Show why the soil reading cannot stand, since the plant still makes food inside itself and the soil is only one of its intakes. Do not repeat the earlier intake-versus-made distinction in full, and do not slide into the water-as-food reading here.\",\\n      \"evidence\": \"The slot\\'s typical intents all treat both sides as legitimate or as a slip; M1 is a held belief that must be surfaced and shut down, so an atypical intent is required and the slot purpose still matches exactly.\",\\n      \"id\": \"contrast-b1\",\\n      \"intent\": \"diagnose-misconception\"\\n    },\\n    \"terminology\": []\\n  },\\n  \"mode\": \"generate\",\\n  \"scoped_request\": {\\n    \"allowed_facts\": [\\n      \"plants have roots, stems and leaves\",\\n      \"living things need food to grow\",\\n      \"Plants are living things that can produce their own food\",\\n      \"Plants are called producers\",\\n      \"Use one concrete classroom example.\",\\n      \"Explain that plants make their own food (are producers)\",\\n      \"Identify leaves as the site of photosynthesis and chlorophyll as the light-capturing substance\",\\n      \"Describe photosynthesis as using light, carbon dioxide, and water to produce food and oxygen\",\\n      \"Explain why light is essential for photosynthesis\"\\n    ],\\n    \"brief\": \"Take the windowsill plant and put the soil-as-food reading beside the target: one learner says the plant\\'s food is the soil, pulled up through the roots; the producer idea says the plant makes its own food and only draws materials from the soil. Show why the soil reading cannot stand, since the plant still makes food inside itself and the soil is only one of its intakes. Do not repeat the earlier intake-versus-made distinction in full, and do not slide into the water-as-food reading here.\",\\n    \"evidence\": \"The slot\\'s typical intents all treat both sides as legitimate or as a slip; M1 is a held belief that must be surfaced and shut down, so an atypical intent is required and the slot purpose still matches exactly.\",\\n    \"kind\": \"paragraph\",\\n    \"